## Verdict: CONFIRMED

**Mechanism (segments.ts:7-8, 59-63):**
```ts
const OPENUI_FENCE = /```openui(?:-lang)?(?:[ \t]+([^\n]*))?\n/g;
const CLOSE_FENCE = /\n```(?:\n|$)/g;
...
const bodyStart = OPENUI_FENCE.lastIndex;
CLOSE_FENCE.lastIndex = bodyStart;
const closing = CLOSE_FENCE.exec(text);
const bodyEnd = closing?.index ?? text.length;
const program = text.slice(bodyStart, bodyEnd);
```
`CLOSE_FENCE` is a bare regex scan over the raw text from `bodyStart` — it has no notion of quoted strings, so the *first* occurrence anywhere of `\n``` ` followed by `\n`/EOF is treated as the closing fence, even if that occurrence sits inside a double-quoted openui-lang string literal.

Compare with `web/node_modules/@openuidev/lang-core/dist/index.mjs`'s `stripFences` (line 2145) and `skipString` (line 2132): the upstream scanner explicitly calls `skipString(input, i)` before testing for a backtick fence (lines 2151-2155 and 2174-2178), so any ``` `"`..."` `` span — including embedded triple-backticks — is skipped wholesale and never mistaken for a fence boundary. Keating's `segments.ts` has no equivalent skip step.

**Concrete trigger** (reproduced with node):
```js
const text = '```openui\nText text="Look:\n```\ncode\n```"\n```\n';
```
i.e. an assistant message containing an `openui` fence whose program has a prop (`text="Look:\n```\ncode\n```"`) that embeds a markdown code sample via triple backticks. Running `parseOpenUIMessageSegments`-equivalent logic on this input:
- `bodyStart` = 10 (right after ```` ```openui\n ````)
- `CLOSE_FENCE.exec` matches at index 26, the `\n```\n` that appears *inside* the quoted prop value (right after `Look:`), not the real closing fence at the end.
- Result: `program === 'Text text="Look:'` — truncated mid-string-literal, `complete: true` is reported even though the real program continues, and the remainder (`code\n```"\n\`\`\`\n`) is emitted as a separate stray `text` segment.

This is an exact match for the candidate finding: a prop value containing a fenced markdown code sample causes premature, incorrect truncation because `CLOSE_FENCE` is not string-literal-aware, unlike upstream's `skipString`-guarded `stripFences`.

